{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Live demonstration of this defect from another session: a reclaim that improved the filesystem by 4.93 GB made `docker_unaccounted_bytes` **4.75 GB worse**. The metric moved decisively in the wrong direction.\n\n**What was done.** `docker builder prune -f` on the production host at ~10:55 UTC, 2026-09-05. Measured immediately before and after with `df -k /` and `docker system df` on the box:\n\n    BEFORE   /dev/nvme0n1p1  62837740  45189568  17648172  72% /\n             Build Cache  107 entries  0 ACTIVE  5.877GB  5.877GB RECLAIMABLE\n\n    AFTER    /dev/nvme0n1p1  62837740  40263832  22573908  65% /\n             Build Cache   25 entries  0 ACTIVE  0B       0B\n\nReal bytes freed on the filesystem: **4,925,736 KB = 4.93 GB**, seven percentage points of root.\n\n**What the metric did.** `docker_unaccounted_bytes` went from ~3.79e9 before to **8.54e9 after** — up ~4.75 GB.\n\n**Why, and why it is exactly this ticket's defect.** The metric is `du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker` (ALLOCATED BLOCKS) minus the four `docker system df` rows (APPARENT sizes). The prune removed 5.877 GB from the LEDGER — the subtrahend — but only 4.93 GB from the disk, the minuend's basis. A ~0.95 GB gap opens immediately, and it opens in the direction that inflates \"unaccounted\", on top of the ~12.4% structural inflation this ticket already documents.\n\nSo the arithmetic is not merely imprecise: **a genuine, successful, 100%-reclaimable cleanup registers as a large regression** in the metric whose name claims to measure bytes no prune can reach. That is close to the inverse of its stated purpose, and it will now keep `rebar-docker-unaccounted-bytes` (threshold 2 GiB) alarming harder than before the host got healthier.\n\n**Operator consequence, since that is what makes this worth a comment rather than a footnote.** An operator watching this metric would read the post-prune value as evidence the prune failed or made things worse, and could reasonably escalate to something more destructive — a `system prune`, an image purge — chasing 8.54 GB of \"unaccounted\" bytes that are substantially an accounting artifact. The correct reading required going to `df` and `docker system df` directly, which is precisely what this ticket says the metric should have spared us.\n\n**Not claiming this is the whole gap.** ~0.95 GB is the ledger-vs-disk discrepancy from this one operation; the rest of the 8.54 GB includes the xfs block-allocation overhead already quantified here (56% of the residue in the earlier measurement) plus whatever genuinely un-ledgered content exists. I did not re-derive the split post-prune.\n\nRecording as evidence only; no action taken on this ticket. Related: `undefiled-revivable-gemsbok` covers the sibling defect where `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` is clamped at 100 and so could not show that the same cache was 9% OVER its 5.00 GiB budget before this prune."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788606605830591152,"uuid":"9f0619c4-b4f3-4117-a9fb-f94733d6c1e3"}